Good evening, New York 14. My name is Jeffrey Montoya, and I'm with the office of Congresswoman Alexandria Acasio Cortez. Thank you for joining our town hall. Just a few housekeeping notes. If you are logged into the Zoom meeting, please use the Q and A feature to submit your questions. We prioritize questions from constituents from New York 14, so please mention your name and neighborhood. Alternatively, you can email your questions to AOC.townhall at mel.house.gov or call 718-662-5970. Our staff is also available to support any technical difficulties. Our team members we monitoring, monitoring the inbox and phone. We're also live on YouTube, Facebook and Twitter. So please feel free to visit our pages for the live stream. Cart open options is being provided by all hands in motion. If you click the link in the chat, it will take you to a website where you can view the transcription. As a reminder to our speakers this evening. Please speak slowly so our interpreters can interpret to the best of their abilities. For today's town hall, we'll hear from the Congresswoman on the latest developments on Capitol Hill, between the nearest shutdown, removal of the speaker of the House of Representatives, our work on migrants in New York City, and a readout of the historic delegation that Congresswoman led to Latin America. Finally, we have some, finally, we have some deadlines and flag for the community. We will then open it up for Q and A from constituents. I would now like to turn it over to Congresswoman Ocasio Cortez. Hello, everybody. Hello, New York 14. This is Congresswoman Alexandria Casio Cortez, here for our town hall, our regular town hall, and we're so excited to be able to run through some of the latest developments in Washington, provide some critical updates, and let you all know what's been going on and give you the opportunity to ask a little bit more about it. Um, you know, we have had a very crazy week in Washington, if not a crazy couple of weeks in Washington, and so I'm going to cover with you, um, briefly how we were able to avoid a government shut down for now, last weekend, how we have a continuing resolution. Uh, that keeps the government spending at current levels, um, and talk about what that presently means. So we have a couple of things that we wanted to chat about, the shutdown, the speaker's race. Additionally, some local issues that we will also dive into. So we'll start very quickly as it pertains to some of the news around government shutdown that you may have heard about last week or so. Um, The way that government funding works and how we normally fund the government to avoid a shutdown is that there are 12 core departments, um, that essentially comprise the United States government when it comes to government funding. These are things like the Department of Labor, the Department of Health and Human Services, the Department of Education, at the Department, you know, the Department of Interior goes on and on. And, um, And all of these bills, and all of these different departments get funded, and that is how we're able to get our social security checks, get title one funding for low income schools and more. And usually, when we have each department get funded. They usually get funded and passed throughout the entire year. And so, for example, in the last couple of years, when Democrats had the House majority, we slowly just passed all of these government funding bills throughout the year, then our bill, which may be slightly different from the Senate's bill, go into conference, the House and Senate hash out their differences. It goes to the president's desk. The president signs it. And our government gets funded, and you usually don't hear too much about it, because it's a pretty standard procedure. Uh, when it comes to our normal government processes. So you might hear about some of the changes that might happen, but rarely are we, is it normal to not fund the government, to miss some of these deadlines, and to enter a shutdown. Um, in fact, the only times that we've gone into shutdown in the last 30 years. Coincidentally happens to be when Republicans have the House majority. Um, it's shut down has never been initiated in the last 30 years when there has had majority. Anyways, all of that is to say, is that this year, under the Republican majority, Republicans fell very far behind. And they had been looking to make really significant cuts to the Social Security Administration, the Department of Education and more. And of course, these are not cuts that Democrats support. And when you have a house majority, Republicans it would be typical for Republicans to pass that. They would have to negotiate with the Senate. They figure out their differences and it goes to the president. What happened a couple of weeks ago was something that was very interesting. Which is that the Republican Party was fighting amongst themselves. So this was not actually a shutdown or a risk of shutdown that happened because the 2 parties couldn't come to an agreement. But it was because the Republican Party's extreme right wing, um, really wanted and demanded some of these cuts, and they did not want, um, They did not want any continuing resolution, any government funding to be to happen on a bipartisan basis. In fact, last Friday, the Republican Party passed what is known as a continuing resolution or a CR. That's kind of like a temporary extension of government funding. That had. 30% cuts almost across the board in many different administrations. Most Republicans voted for it, um, including our New York State, uh, Republicans as well. And so they tried to pass 30% cuts to the social security administration, 80% cuts to Title one low income schools, which are many of the schools in our community and others. But they weren't able to pass that. And they didn't have the votes to pass it. And so on Saturday, what they essentially did was pass a continuing resolution, that essentially was a clean extension, and normally what happens with a vehicle like this, is that you take whatever the current government funding is, and you say, okay, we're just going to extend everything at its current level without any changes, um, for X period of time. And while we sort out our differences. And that is what happened. On Saturday, we filed an extension of the current government funding levels for just 45 days so that federal workers can get their paychecks, um, and the government can continue to be funded and to can continue to function. While folks over there settle out their differences. For context in our community, we have over 5000 federal workers. We have LaGuardia Airport in our community in the larger, broader context of New York City. Our airport workers, our military service members and many others, are federal employees. And if we shut down the government. And if the government shuts down, then the 1st people that, um, that miss a paycheck, can be our government workers and our federal workers. And there's no reason or rationale that everyday ordinary people would have to suffer in the event because of a political squabble. And that's normally why a CR happens and is extended. And it's always been very important, at least to us, to try to avoid a government shut down, because it's just not fair and it's just not right to put people in the middle of that. All of that is to say. Is that on Saturday, we had we were able to achieve a 45 day extension, but the way that we did that was with Democrats and Republicans working together. In fact, more Democrats voted to keep the government open than Republicans did on Saturday. And that set up a series of events that basically led to what we see this week, which was the removal of the speaker of the house. Now, I want to be very clear that, um, this removal was not particularly because Democrats uh, worked with Republicans. This was, I think, a long time coming because of a series of agreements that the former speaker had essentially agreed to. Um, in January of this year, the Speaker of the House and the Republican Majority Party agreed to change the underlying rules of the House of Representatives. And there's always been something known as a motion to vacate. And this is almost been like an emergency, smash the glass in case something happens, uh, and something an extreme emergency around the speaker of the of the house occurs, and the speaker must be removed. This is typically a very high threshold. In order to implement a motion to vacate historically, you must essentially have huge, huge droves, um, of members of the House of Representatives to remove the speaker. And this is precisely because this is such a serious measure that removal is not something that should be politicized or weaponized. Um, However, in January, that was our take. And in January of this year, however, the Republican Party disagreed, and they changed the underlying house rules to allow just one member to file a motion to vacate. And if just one member initiates a moment, a motion to vacate, then within 48 hours, the House of Representatives must, um, cast a vote. And If that vote yields yes on the motion to vacate, then the speaker is consequently removed. Now in January, every single Democrat voted against changing that rule. We said, This is not only large break from precedent, but it's probably not good for you all to set this precedent for yourselves. And every single Democrat voted against this rule change. However, Every single Republican voted for it. And when Republicans have the house majority, and when they have control of the house, when every single Republican votes for something, it will happen. That is the nature of a majority. And so the speaker of the House and the Republican Party insisted on a one person motion to vacate while they were in the majority. And that is what was implemented. And almost this entire year, the speaker of the house has been quite beholden to this extremist wing of the party, because this motion to vacate that he adopted and approved had been kind of looming over his head. Now, on top of that, we've also seen a series of destabilizing moves, uh, within the institution of the speaker of the house. The current speaker, or the former speaker, Kevin McCarthy, he voted to overturn the results of the U.S. presidential election. Additionally, he held the entire U.S. economy hostage earlier this year, and, um, and he, by refusing to raise the U.S. debt limit, uh, to pay for spending that he himself had already approved of. And also that the House had already voted on. In fact, having a debt limit separate from our budgets is a very strange and bizarre archaic mechanism that does not exist in most countries. And so, Kevin McCarthy, by holding the entire US economy hostage in March, struck a deal with President Biden. And that's in that deal. That's one of the reasons why we have student loan repayments that are restarting. And in addition to cuts to certain critical services. And regardless of whether we agree with that or not, I'm vehemently again, I was vehemently against it. Um, and voted against it. But whether we agree with that or not. At the end of the day, that is the deal that was struck to keep the U.S. government open. Now President Biden has upheld his end of the deal. He has exacted and executed those concessions that were issued unfairly, but that were issued in order to keep the government open. And one of the problems is that Kevin McCarthy has not. And so Biden gave his end of the deal. And in this most recent CR, Kevin McCarthy said, never mind. And this has created a situation that has largely become untenable. And so on Tuesday, Monday or Tuesday. Uh, one of the members of the Republican caucus filed a motion to vacate. Now, I want to be very clear that Pretty much never in the history of the institution has one political party voted for the other party speaker. Democrats do not elect Republican speakers and Republicans have never elected Democratic speakers. This is almost a foundational part of the institution. And in fact, It's part of having a party majority. It is, if Republicans are elected in the majority, it is up to Republicans to elect a Republican speaker. And when Democrats are in the majority, it is up to Democrats to elect a Democratic speaker. And uh, what happened this week was that Republicans cast this vote. Uh, Kevin McCarthy exhibited extraordinary confidence that he would win this vote. He told everyone to bring it on. He said that he did not need to negotiate with Democrats. He did not ask Democrats for votes. He did not indicate in any single way, shape, or form that he would need Democratic votes. And so the vote happened. And the votes that Kevin McCarthy had publicly suggested that he had, did not follow through within his Republican caucus. And when that happens, that means he lost the motion to vacate, and he was removed as speaker of the house. This is the 1st time that this has ever happened in US history. The last time a motion to vacate even occurred was over a 100 years ago, and that motion, I believe, failed. And so, This is the 1st time in U.S. history, the speaker of the house has been removed and vacated. This also means that the line of succession to the presidency has a hole in it. Usually, the line of succession is the president. If the president cannot serve his duties. The vice president, and after the vice president, it's the speaker of the House. Currently, there is no elected speaker of the house. There is, there's kind of a placeholder, known as a speaker pro tempore, but a speaker pro tempore is not does not qualify. Um, to be able to serve in the line of succession. And so right now, we have the president, the vice president, the gap, and then in what goes after that gap is the current head of the Senate, the, um, the eldest serving member of the Senate in the majority, which is Senator Patty Murray. And so that is the current situation that we are in. The House Republican caucus is in a bit of chaos right now, they have the responsibility of nominating their party speaker. Democrats, we will be, I will be going back to Washington on Tuesday, and Democrats will nominate their nominee for speaker, which, in our case, is fellow New Yorker, Representative Hakeem Jeffries of Brooklyn. And on Tuesday, uh, the, at least it's currently scheduled that on Tuesday, they're going to be the nominating conference meetings. So Republicans will put forward and select their conference nominee who the name that they are going to submit to the house floor, and then Democrats are going to select their name that they're going to submit to the house floor. We know who that Democrat is going to be. We do not yet know who that Republican is going to be. Until then, there is an interim speaker. That interim speaker, really cannot. Let's see, the interim speaker can only really do kind of clerical duties. They cannot bring any legislation to the floor. We cannot vote on any bills. We cannot do anything until we select a new speaker. And currently, the one individual that by far has the most votes. On the floor is Democrat Hakeem Jeffries. And now he has about 212 Democratic votes. However, you have to get 218 or so, um, in order to clinch the speakership. And so, We don't know who the Republican nominee is. One viable option is that if, Somewhere between 6 or 7, 6 to 8, um, you know, more moderate district Republicans, I won't say moderate Republicans, but moderate districts, Republicans. And join, uh, the Democratic caucus, That is one way we can end through this. There could be a coalition candidate. Uh, but we are in a standstill. And the clock is ticking during this standstill because we have that 45 day government funding extension. And so the government has only been is was only authorized to be funded for 45 days starting last Saturday. We've lost a week on this. We will likely lose another week next week because Republicans have not determined who their leadership is going to be. And so the clock is slowly ticking on that, we have about mid-November, I believe November 15th or so is when this clock runs out. I do not anticipate that given all of this chaos over there that Republicans will be able to pass those 12 departmental bills that I discussed earlier, but one option that is on the table is a 2nd continuing resolution, just like the one that we just passed that gave us a 45 day extension. Continuing resolutions can last as long as. in, you know, at almost as long as we want them to within a congressional term. Uh, but that is our current dilemma. And that's currently where we are at in terms of the speakership. I also want to provide a quick update on the migration situation in New York City. Last time we spoke. I had indicated that I had been fighting alongside other members of the New York Democratic, um, or rather the New York Congressional delegation to secure something known as TPS for Venezuelan migrants. Now, the vast majority of migrants or the largest group of migrants that have been arriving in New York City over the last several years have come from Venezuela. We've discussed some of the reasons why, including the contributions that U.S. sanctions, broad-based U.S. sanctions have been playing in destabilizing the Venezuelan economy, which is really putting a lot of poor and working families into poverty and destitution and forcing them to flee to other countries, not just the United States, but also Colombia, Chile, and more. And so in addition to that's kind of an aside, but one of the key things that we've also been talking about, with respect to those migrants that are here, is in order to ease the strain on our shelter systems and public systems, they have to be able to work. And asylum seekers that have come to the United States historically have not been allowed to work. And so they're not allowed to work. They are not allowed to sustain themselves. And so they're forced to remain in our shelters and forced to rely on our public systems, even when they don't want to. In fact, when I visited, when I have visited our shelter system several times, and every time I speak to these folks, the number one thing that they say is, I just want to work, just let me work and let me get on my feet and let me provide for my kids. And on the other end of that equation, one of the other things that we hear from a lot of industries in the city and across the state of New York is, let us hire them, especially the agricultural industry, the hospitality industry. They say we really need New Yorkers. We have, and we really need these new New Yorkers. And we really need to be able to hire these folks because we have not been able to fill this labor need up until this point. And so we have folks that want to hire. We have folks that want to work. But the federal government was kind of putting up a lot of red tape and wasn't allowing people to work. And so I've been pushing alongside the New York congressional delegation for over a year to make sure that we secured something known as TPS or temporary protected status. For Venezuelans, and not only Venezuelans, but folks from many other countries as well. And just last month, after pushing and pushing and pushing, Biden administration on this, we finally had a breakthrough. And we were able to secure temporary protected status for Venezuelan asylum seekers here in the United States, and that has been a major boon for the current situation. That means over or about half a 1000000 asylum seekers will now be able to get on a pathway to be allowed to work, which will hopefully really reduce the strain on our systems and also help us fill our economic needs that we have in the city. Uh, leading up to that. I led a delegation alongside congressman and espayat of members of the congressional Hispanic caucus so we could get members everywhere from California to Illinois and Massachusetts to New York City to take a look at our system. And they actually were very impressed with some of the things that they saw. So that's what we got there on that one. And then during August. One of the things that I did was I led one of the 1st ever, if not the 1st ever, all Spanish speaking, U.S. congressional delegation to a South American, um, Codell, a South American delegation. And there we met with the presidential administrations of President Lula of Brazil. I also met with President Gabi Borich of Chile, as well as President Petro of Colombia. And during the that congressional delegation, we were able to engage in really serious diplomatic conversations regarding climate, protection of the Amazon, acknowledgment of the US role in the 1973 Chilean coup, and figuring out how we can fight against, um, uh, forces that are undermining democracy across Latin America. And with respect to Colombia, we also engaged additionally on discussions around climate as well as US drug war policy. And how we can continue to support peace efforts in the country. Um, it was a profoundly helpful trip. Uh, as well as the fact that we connected with US embassy teams and our ambassadors in the region to help figure out how we can start addressing some of the root causes of the migraine crisis. Because, in my opinion and in my view, no matter what we do around enforcement and deterrence, et cetera. The core of the problem is the fact that 1000000s of people are pouring out of countries and being displaced in the 1st place. And we will continue to have challenges with respect to migration, if we continue to support policies that drive displacement and mass migration in the 1st place. A lot of these people. Are not coming for normal reasons. They are being displaced from their homes, and one of the best immigration and asylum seeking solutions is to make sure that people don't have to be refugees in the 1st place. And it's an important point for us to reassess how the U.S. engages in Latin America and supports stability in the region and peace in the region. and economic dignity in the region, so that we don't have to be overwhelmed, and that our neighbors don't have to be overwhelmed either. Um, in terms of the actual numbers, the United States has about half a 1000000 um, Venezuelan immigrants within our borders, but Colombia has taken 2 to 3000000 compared to the 500,000 that are currently in the United States. And so I hope that gives a little bit of a scale, uh, in terms of how we all need to come together as a hemisphere to support policies that make people not want to leave and flee their homes in the 1st place. And at the end of the day, the more that we do that, the more we'll be able to support what everybody wants to do. And then on the other end, when people do come. to support pathways to citizenship, make it easier to be documented because we have an enormous economic need, as well as enormous cultural need in the United States. There are states that have extremely xenophobic and anti-immigrant policies, and in fact, because they have those policies, while they are in a labor shortage, they are rolling back child labor laws in some of these states because they are so desperate for healthy economy where people can work. But what is happening is that children are getting harmed. There are reports of deaths in certain facilities. And that is simply not an acceptable answer to our solution. We have to be able to put these 2 pieces together while also supporting stability and dignity abroad. And I think that if we're able to aggressively pursue that solution, while also continuing to push for immigration reform, justice for our DACA recipients, a path to citizenship, for the undocumented, that have established their lives here, then we can make significant progress in a way that helps everyone in this country. And so with that, we will pursue and follow up on some reminders for you all in the community. 1st thing that I would say is