to the congressional app challenge. And on top of that, our service academy nomination deadline. The overall service academy nomination deadline is on November 1st. So is the congressional app challenge. Those are folks who are interested in applying to the US Service Academies. That is the US Merchant Marine Academy, West Point, Air Force, Naval Academy, Arlington, et cetera. And those are the service academies. So people sometimes will choose to elect to go to those institutions. They are colleges and they are also a military service academy. So if you are interested in applying, if that is part of your plan, that deadline to seek a nomination, which is required. is November 1st and you can submit those applications to my office. And I think that's all. Thank you so much. Thank you for your remarks, Congresswoman. We will now move to the Q and A portion of our town hall. As mentioned earlier, those signed on via the Zoom platform may use the Q and A feature to submit your questions. Please, please mention what neighborhood you are from as you will prioritize New York 14 constituents. Okay. Uh, Jennifer Torres from Hunt's point asks. I can see the disparity between the Hunts Point community and others in the borough when it comes to access to quality and healthy food choices. Our neighborhood only has 2 major supermarkets who price gouge whenever they can. I live in the supermarket wasteland. What can we do to be able to have better shopping choices in our community? Thank you so much. Um, Jennifer, for your question, it is incredibly important. And health disparities and health inequity is a major issue that we're always trying to tackle in our community. It's something that the Bronx in particular, um, has long had, uh, encountered inequalities around and it's something that's been very important to us. In fact, earlier this year. We've been looking for dramatic expansions to an investment in the Hunts Point produce market. And our hope is that those investments in that market will also make it easier for the fresh goods in that market to be able to be sustained and distributed locally. In terms of federal programming around this. The USDA, that's the US Department of Agriculture, is currently has a healthy food financing initiative. And what the goal of that healthy food financing initiative is, is to provide business assistance, loans, grants as well, um, to help improve access to fresh foods. And right now, what we're doing is that we are working in DC, especially under, um, the upcoming agriculture, the agricultural bill, but in many others as well, um, to support expanding that program so that we can maintain new grocery stores in our neighborhood. Um, and especially those neighborhoods that are food deserts and don't have access to fresh foods. Now, we have challenges with that in a Republican majority, but it is something that we're continually trying to elevate as well as working with our city and state partners to address. But the food disparity and health disparities are really are very real. And a lot of the causes are very structural. And so part of what we're trying to do is really increase the financial incentives, uh, to keep grocery stores open and to make sure that those folks who want to open them can open them in neighborhoods where they are most needed. Tanya Smith from co-op city asks, are you in agreement with congestion, congestion pricing, and why? Isn't there another attacks in the outer boroughs? In my area, public transportation is not the best and our concerns are not answered. Thank you so much for the question. Now, I think when it comes to congestion pricing, it's all about the design of congestion pricing, I think it's very important that, you know, for me, there are certain principles that I am in agreement with. I think one of the major parts of congestion pricing is when it comes to commuters from out of state that are coming into, um, that are coming into New York City and Manhattan, using our infrastructure, but not contributing to our public taxes. I think it, I am in support of congestion pricing. However, I also want to um, note that we have long uh, said that we want to make sure that congestion pricing, does not amplify environmental injustices, and also can address existing inequities with our local communities in the Bronx and Queens. Lately, there has been some conversation about some of the ways that congestion pricing could roll out. I believe the transit workers alliance, you know, the funds for congestion pricing are originally supposed to invest in public transit, so that public transit can both be more frequent and expanded into places that are transit deserts. I think that's the right way to go about it. I think making sure that we don't harm already underserved communities is the most is a critical and important priority in support for congestion pricing. And so for me, it's all about the design and making sure that we are not harming our existing communities. And so we're currently monitoring the situation. Again, I believe, I believe in a core, in, on the base idea, that if you are coming from out of state, and using our infrastructure, then a congestion price is appropriate, um, and, and I think that, that the folks who are using the roads most heavily, you know, pay, uh, a congestion tool, but at the same time, we want to make sure that those are not accelerating environmental injustices in the Bronx, and that we aren't making it, uh, regressive and more difficult for communities that have already been most impacted. Blake Valker from a story asks. Now that there is no speaker, are we looking at a shutdown in November? Should I start figuring out how to lower some of my expenses so I have some money on the side to help them if it starts to get untenable? Well, you know, again, I am so sorry that we even have to have this conversation. It is not something that should even be a reality. And frankly, as you know, has not been a reality. Recently. Of course, there was a government shutdown during the Trump administration, but in the last several years, there haven't been. It just simply has not been an issue. Now, in terms of saying, will there or won't there be a shutdown in November? I, unfortunately, cannot give you a guarantee either way. Because, as you noted, of this speakership debacle. Now, our hope, and we are fighting tooth and nail, just like we fought tooth and nail last Saturday to avoid a shutdown, but at the end of the day, we cannot avoid a shutdown without Republicans and without Republican, other Republican ability to use their majority to help us avoid a shutdown. And so what I would personally say would be to prepare for the fact that this is a possibility. Now, the vast majority of government shutdowns have not lasted a long period of time. In fact, the largest bulk of them, have been about a week. But once again, during the Trump administration. That that precedent was broken. And that shutdown lasts about 35 days. It was the longest one in American history. I of course, do not anticipate that. We hope that we would, that this would, that that would never happen again. Um, but, I have to be completely honest and say, I cannot guarantee that there will not be one, unfortunately, because, um, Democrats don't have the votes to keep it open right now. We need Republicans to meet us in the middle, um, and make sure that we can get paychecks to people. John Schwam from City Island asks, I think all residents of the East Bronx would like to see the roadways of Pelham Bay Park and its Hutchison River Parkway Access Roads upgraded to state and federal department of transportation standards, including the needed flood mitigation on park roads. Is it possible to use federal coastal resiliency funds to accelerate this? It's an excellent, um, it's a really excellent question. It's certainly something when it comes to coastal resiliency that we've been looking into with respect to uh, projects like Orchard Beach and other areas around Pelham Bay Park. We have examined some of the federal opportunities there. We do have significant community outreach to us around flooding and I'm just taking a look at some notes here. We do have had some significant community outreach when it comes to the flooding on shore road specifically. And so I want to thank you all for engaging us on this issue because government can only be responsive when people speak up and reach out. And so we've had a lot of people reaching out to us about the flooding on shore road, and we've actually coordinated with the city and the state to, I start identifying some potential federal grants, such as the surface transportation block grant. And so that has been one of the main vehicles that we have already begun to identify to help fix the flooding in some of these areas. And I'll repeat that again. It is the surface transportation block grant that we've identified that we're now starting to work with the city and state to see if we can secure and what we can secure for shore road and some of those surrounding areas as well. Coastal resilience grants do tend to be more environmental protection measures. So that is a little less road focused, those tend to be around shoring up more natural land, and preventing erosion when we do see some of that flooding. And flood resiliency grants focus on buildings. So flood resiliency is buildings, coastal resilience is general wildland and surface transportation is, I think, what we can hone in on and what we're already starting to explore when it comes to helping out and upgrading our roads, which are absolutely in need of a lot of investment and help. And so if we're able to secure that funding, The work is usually completed by the state and the city, so we get the money and the city and the state, deploy it, and it depends on the jurisdiction as well. So those entities would be responsible for applying the funding, for the funding, and we can support the city of the state, and we've already started in supporting the city and state in applying for those funds. Robert Morerer of College Point asks, what is going on with the migrants being placed within your district? Specifically college point. Thank you for the question, Robert. Currently there are several shelters in the district. Uh, spanning, and again, the district goes, it expands, it spans across 2 boroughs. We've got the Bronx, we've got parts of the Bronx, and then we've got parts of Queens. So there are several shelters in within that larger area, and there are 2 respite centers. Respite centers are kind of like these large centers where there are some cots, people can rest while they seek shelter and seek placement as well. Um, the site in college point. St. Agnes is a respite center, and that is operated by the New York City emergency management, and it will actually close at the end of the month on October 31st. Um, we hear that the city may have plans to turn it into a school, but we have to wait for any of those things to be made official. Um, but again, there's currently a respite center in college point that will be closing at the end of the month. Additionally, the city has moved to limit shelter stays for single men to 30 days. Um, This is pretty consistent when, at least when it comes to the data around single men, uh, the majority of asylum seekers who are single men are usually in and out of our system, um, in less than 30 days that we have seen. Um, but no one wants to see people sleeping in cots in these small congregate settings like these. I've been in them that think of them almost as like a large gym or a large space. And they are just those cops that you see that are there and people just bring whatever it is, whatever belongings that they have a blanket, a bag. Um, and it is just a place to put their head down for the night and have protection from the elements. Um, as they try to figure out their application and, uh, and their path forward. And our hope is that the half a 1000000 TPS designations that we were successful in opening up can help people more quickly get on their feet. Alexis Sussman of Elmhurst asks, what is the future for any aid for student loans, not only for federal student loans assistance, but also for private student loans? So student loans. Massive developments happening this week. I will start on, um, the 1st uh, aspect of this, which is what we've seen is that, um, we've got federal student loan payments that have restarted this month, October 1st, uh, and I want to be very clear, if you've missed that deadline right now, that was the 1st payment deadline. There will not be credit reporting for 12 months. Actually, I think it's actually closer to 15 months. So if you've missed a payment as these payments go online, credits will your credit score will not be dinged if you miss a payment. This is to help people. Um get back on board. If your payment is really high, there are currently new programs that have rolled out called the save program. Um, and uh, we've linked to some of these things on social media. And we have a, you know, information about this. And the save program, if you make $15 an hour, or if you also make other income thresholds, your student loan payment could be brought down to zero. Um, so I would definitely look into that if you need to. Even though your your payment will not be dinged on your credit, if you miss it, interest does start accruing at that date, October 1st. So I would definitely look into that, make sure that you have that short up. As for the future of student loan cancellation. This week, President Biden announced an additional $9000000000 in student loan cancellation. There are also other student loan cancellation programs from public student loan forgiveness, public service, loan forgiveness, to teacher loan forgiveness, disability, loan forgiveness, that, um, we've also uploaded information about that I encourage you all to explore and research. And now moving forward forward. President Biden and the administration. We've been working with them. And after the Supreme Court overturned student loan forgiveness. What we examined and what we sought was to find another way to pursue student loan cancellation that does not conflict with the specific law that the Supreme Court pointed out in their ruling, which was the Heroes Act. We want to cancel it through the higher education act. And so right now, uh, President Biden and the administration, uh, along with ourselves, have been pushing the administration on rule making, uh, for to announce additional loan forgiveness. Um, During that rule making period, that might take about, I'd say 10 months or so, uh, from around now. And so I do believe that we're going to hear more about student loan forgiveness over the next year or so, but we don't have the specific details from the administration yet. As soon as we get those details from the administration, we will absolutely keep folks updated. To your question, however, these are all federal programs because that is what is under federal jurisdiction, that is what the government is able to do is, especially on the presidential administration level, is able to work, um, they have jurisdiction, they have control over public and publicly subsidized student loans, federal student loans. As for private student loans, that is much more challenging, uh, for, of the president, the president's administration to be able to address because they aren't under federal jurisdiction. Those are private companies. And so, as far as those go, that would likely need, perhaps some sort of legislative, uh, fix or some sort of consumer protection, um, action in terms of being a watchdog on any potential bad actors, and we're happy to go and see what we can find out in terms of options on private loans, which are, again, a bit more challenging to regulate than federal loans. Patricia from Parkchester asks, why are you against building a border wall? Excellent question. Thank you, Patricia. One of the things that we have seen from the data. Now, border walls have been built, not just under the previous administration, but they have been built for decades, frankly. And what we largely see is that, um, our issue with these walls is that they aren't actually not very strong deterrence. What they do do is drive a lot of people into the most rural areas who then often pass away or succumb to the elements. This is a humanitarian crisis. Um, And moreover, one of the things that we're seeing is that the largest concerns, the things that people try to avoid most, when it comes to building a wall, are things like the importation of dangerous substances like fentanyl, for example, but actually, the vast majority of fentanyl is going through the existing checkpoints and they're being imported by Americans. And so many of the primary issues that people are trying to address, uh, with a wall are not actually addressed by a wall. It doesn't actually solve our problems. Um, and it doesn't solve the problems that people claim that building a wall would solve. Now, what does help are investments in science, technology, and what helps the most is an immigration system that does not incentivize people coming by foot. One of the reasons why we are having this issue in the 1st place is because existing US policy deeply incentivizes a lot of people to come by land. And to come by foot. And a lot of these people would prefer to use other modes of transportation and other modes of access. And so I think our primary priority is to have is to reform our immigration system, update our visa system. Um, and also allow people to apply for asylum and be able to be initially approved. A lot our, the way our current system operates is that it forces people to show up at ports of entry in person on land in order to apply. And so, um, we want to be able, of course, to preserve the right to do that, seeking asylum is a human right. Um, but also we want to expand people's ability to do that as well. And so for me, I think it's just that we can take those resources and use them much more effectively to actually solve the problem. In addition to the fact that there's actually an enormous amount of environmental damage done as well. There are migratory species, there are so many other issues, as well as US citizens who live on the border. Um, their homes can be damaged, there can be eminent domain issues. People live there, land crosses there, and these are also people's homes too. And so even just logistically, financially, um, it's not a viable solution. It's also extremely expensive requires a huge amount of maintenance and is often ineffective. Carlos from Corona is asking. Our street vendors are still out of work. It has been 2 months since they were out of work because most of them were displaced by the NYPD. I would like to know if the government or the city is working on a solution for street vendors. Many of them do not have working papers to access a regular job. They're essential for our economy and social environment. We want a solution because this is not only affecting their out income, but also the future of their children. Mm-hmm. Thank you so much for this question. It cuts to a really important issue. First and foremost, one of the things that we need to do is lean on the city to expand the amount of street vendor permits that are allowed. Our city has not increased the level of permits, the level of the number of people that we legally allow to operate food businesses or sell things in a sanctioned way, um, in a very, very long, long time. And so the number is extremely low. And that forces um, a lot of people to operate without licenses that they are qualified to have and that they would be happy to have if they were available. So we have been very supportive of raising the number of licenses, but I actually do have an update specifically around the Corona Plaza situation. We have been working very closely with the street vendors project in addition to the borough president and local elected officials, like assembly woman, Jennifer Gonzalez Rojas, Catalina Cruz, and more. And uh, what the borough president, um, stated today or yesterday was that he's going to start really pushing for this in a significant way. Right now, as some of you may know, um, there is a NYCFC, the soccer stadium, uh, that, um, some folks are trying to build out in bullets point, the borough president recently said that he will not authorize that to continue until, uh, the city provides a solution for uh, for vendors and for Corona Plaza. And so our hope is that that will create enough political pressure to get the city, to allow people to be permitted, to operate their business, um, in a way that is sustainable and healthy for everybody in the entire community. Corona Plaza is has historically been one of the top culinary destinations in the world. And that is because of our immigrant community. We want to make sure that people can operate it in a way that is healthy, happy above board and supports our local families. Thank you, Congresswoman, for your responses. Unfortunately, we run out of time. Thank you so much to our constituents for asking these questions. You can always contact our office by calling 718-662-5970 representative Ocasio Cortez. Uh, thank you so much to our constituents for joining us. Your engagement is what allows us to continue the fight for you in Congress. I also want to take our transcribers, John Trujillo, and Wendy Brewer. Thank you to our interpreters, Anise Velasquez, Correct, Ruben Martinez, Betsarif, and Lisa Dennett. Again, you can always contact our offices by calling 718-662-5970. Have a good evening, everyone. Thank you, everyone.
